<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prometheus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prometheus is a Titan of Greek mythology who defied the Olympian gods by stealing fire and giving it to humanity; in several versions he also created humans from clay. The oldest record of him is in the late 8th-century BCE poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a>, where he is a son of the Titan Iapetus and a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> and Epimetheus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> punished him by binding him to a rock, where an eagle ate his liver, which grew back overnight; in several versions, most notably Hesiod's, the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> eventually freed him. His cult centred mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and in the Romantic era he came to stand for the lone genius, as in The Modern Prometheus, the alternate title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> (1818).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prometheus.jpg" alt="Prometheus" width="330" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prometheus_and_Atlas,_Laconian_black-figure_kylix,_by_the_Arkesilas_Painter,_560-550_BC,_inv._16592_-_Museo_Gregoriano_Etrusco_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC01069.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Greeks Colchis was Aea, from the 5th century BCE onwards the home of Aeëtes, Medea and the Golden Fleece, the goal of Jason and the Argonauts; here <b>Prometheus</b> was chained to a mountain for revealing fire to humanity, and here some placed the homeland of the Amazons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the origin of the world and the gods, from Chaos and Earth to the overthrow of the Titans by Zeus, who establishes his just rule; his Works and Days teaches the justice of Zeus and tells the story of <b>Prometheus</b>, who stole fire for humanity, and of Pandora.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
